<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Hidden-unit shrinkage · gltbnn</title>
  <meta name="description" content="An interactive mathematical companion to the G, GL and GLT hidden-unit priors for Bayesian neural network regression.">
  <link rel="icon" href="../assets/mark.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
  <script defer src="network.js"></script><script defer src="plots.js"></script><script defer src="hierarchy.js"></script><script defer src="app.js"></script>
</head>
<body><a class="skip-link" href="#topics">Skip to the mathematical explorer</a><div class="shell">
<header class="masthead"><a href="../" class="brand"><img class="brandmark" src="../assets/mark.svg" alt=""><span>gltbnn</span></a>
<nav class="links"><a href="../">R package</a><a href="../prediction/">Fitted example</a><a href="#sources">References</a></nav></header>
<main><header class="hero"><h1>Shrinkage in a Bayesian neural network</h1>
<p>Select unit \(k\) in hidden layer \(l\). Its highlighted connections form one vector \(\mathbf w_{kl}\). The three priors differ in how they assign its scale \(\lambda_{kl}\).</p></header>
<nav class="tabbar" id="topics" aria-label="Mathematical explorer" role="tablist">
@tabs@
</nav><noscript><p>Each hidden unit shares one scale across its incoming weights and its bias. G uses \(\lambda_{kl}=\tau_l\), GL uses \(\lambda_{kl}=\tau_l r_{kl}\), and GLT uses \(\lambda_{kl}\mid\tau_l,\xi_l\sim\operatorname{GPD}(\tau_l,\xi_l)\). The <a href="../reference.pdf">R reference manual</a> gives the full hierarchies. Enable JavaScript to explore them interactively.</p></noscript>
<div class="lab-grid" id="explorer"><section class="network-panel" aria-labelledby="network-title">
<span class="section-label">Network architecture · Figure 3</span><div class="panel-top"><h2 id="network-title">The incoming weight vector \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\)</h2></div>
<div class="network-wrap"><svg id="network" aria-label="Selectable regression network with two hidden layers"></svg></div>
<div class="network-summary" aria-live="polite"><strong id="selection-name"></strong><div id="selected-vector" class="selected-math"></div><span id="selection-detail"></span></div>
<details class="details-box" id="unit-path"><summary>From unit [[k]] in layer [[l]] to the output</summary>
<p id="path-pruned" hidden>The pruning example removes this unit. The equations below describe its connections before removal.</p>
<div data-in-layer="1">
<p>The three predictors feed unit [[k]] in layer 1. The edge from the square input \(1\) carries \(b_{[[kl]]}\), the last entry of \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\). ReLU takes the positive part of the weighted sum.</p>
\[h_{[[kl]]}(\mathbf x)=\max\left\{0,\,b_{[[kl]]}+\sum_{j=1}^{3}w_{[[kl]],j}x_j\right\}.\]
<p>Each of the eight units in layer 2 receives \(h_{[[kl]]}(\mathbf x)\). In unit \(j\), the term from this connection is \(w_{j2,[[k]]}\,h_{[[kl]]}(\mathbf x)\). The eight second-layer activations then enter the output. Shrinking this one first-layer unit leaves each predictor connected through the other three units.</p>
</div>
<div data-in-layer="2" hidden>
<p>The four first-layer activations feed unit [[k]] in layer 2. The edge from the square input \(1\) carries \(b_{[[kl]]}\), the last entry of \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\). ReLU takes the positive part of the weighted sum.</p>
\[h_{[[kl]]}(\mathbf x)=\max\left\{0,\,b_{[[kl]]}+\sum_{j=1}^{4}w_{[[kl]],j}h_{j1}(\mathbf x)\right\}.\]
<p>The outgoing edge carries \(v_{[[k]]}\). This unit adds \(v_{[[k]]}h_{[[kl]]}(\mathbf x)\) to the output.</p>
</div>
\[f_{\mathcal W}(\mathbf x)=b_o+\sum_{j=1}^{8}v_jh_{j2}(\mathbf x).\]
<p>The output vector contains eight weights and its bias \(b_o\). They share a separate scale \(\kappa\), with \(\mathbf v\mid\kappa\sim\mathcal N(\mathbf0,\kappa^2\mathbf I_9)\) and \(\kappa\sim C^+(0,5)\).</p></details>
</section><aside class="inspector" aria-label="Equations and controls">
@panel spread@
<span class="section-label">Gaussian regression · Equation (16)</span><header class="inspector-head"><h2>Conditional weight prior for \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\)</h2></header>
<div class="law">\[\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\mid\lambda_{[[kl]]},\sigma^2\sim\mathcal N(\mathbf0,\sigma^2\lambda_{[[kl]]}^2\mathbf I_{[[d]]}).\]</div>
<p>Unit [[k]] in layer [[l]] has \(d_{[[l]]}=K_{[[prev]]}+1=[[inputs]]+1=[[d]]\) incoming coefficients, including \(b_{[[kl]]}\). Each has conditional standard deviation \(\sigma\lambda_{[[kl]]}\), and they are independent given \(\lambda_{[[kl]]}\) and \(\sigma^2\). The same \(\sigma^2\) is the observation variance in the network likelihood</p>
\[Y_i\mid\mathbf x_i,\mathcal W,\sigma^2\sim\mathcal N(f_{\mathcal W}(\mathbf x_i),\sigma^2).\]
<details class="details-box"><summary>Prior on the observation variance</summary><p>The study uses \(p(\sigma^2)\propto1/\sigma^2\) on the standardized response scale. Posterior propriety holds for almost every response direction under the paper’s conditions. For a given data set, a finite variational fit alone cannot establish propriety. To use the supplement’s proper inverse-gamma alternative in R, set <code>noise_prior = c(shape = 2, scale = 1)</code>, or supply other positive hyperparameters.</p></details>
<div class="range-row"><label for="spread">Observation-error SD \(\sigma\)</label><output for="spread" id="spread-value"></output></div>
<input type="range" id="spread" min=".25" max="2" step=".05" value="1">
<svg id="bell-chart" class="chart" aria-label="Conditional Gaussian density for one coordinate"></svg><p class="readout" id="spread-readout"></p>
<p class="tiny">Both axes stay fixed as \(\sigma\) changes. Changing \(\lambda_{[[kl]]}\) rescales the axes. The tick labels and interval report the spread of one of this unit’s [[d]] coefficients.</p></section>
@panel scales@
<span class="section-label">Conditional scales · Equation (<span id="scale-equation-number">18</span>)</span><header class="inspector-head"><h2>Scale sharing in layer [[l]]</h2></header>
@priors@
<div class="law" id="prior-explainer">@scalelaws@<p class="tiny" id="scale-substitution"></p></div>
<div class="range-row"><label for="layer-scale">Layer scale \(\tau_{[[l]]}\)</label><output for="layer-scale" id="layer-scale-value"></output></div>
<input type="range" id="layer-scale" min=".25" max="1.25" step=".05" value=".5">
<div id="percentile-control"><div class="range-row"><label for="percentile">Quantile \(u\) for unit [[k]]</label><output for="percentile" id="percentile-value"></output></div>
<input type="range" id="percentile" min="10" max="80" step="1" value="60" aria-describedby="percentile-hint">
<p class="tiny" id="percentile-hint">The slider sets \(\lambda_{[[kl]]}=F_{[[l]]}^{-1}(u)\), where \(F_{[[l]]}\) is the conditional unit-scale distribution in layer [[l]].</p></div>
<h3>Conditional weight SDs in layer [[l]]</h3>
<div class="scale-list" id="unit-bars" role="group" aria-label="Conditional standard deviations by hidden unit"></div>
<p class="tiny" id="bars-caption"></p></section>
@panel hierarchy@
<span class="section-label">Prior representations · Figure 4</span><header class="inspector-head"><h2>Two hierarchies for \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\)</h2></header>
@priors@
<div class="choice-group hierarchy-choice" role="group" aria-label="Prior representation"><button type="button" class="choice" data-form="original" aria-pressed="true">Original</button><button type="button" class="choice" data-form="expanded" aria-pressed="false">Parameter expansion</button></div>
<div class="hierarchy-box"><h3 id="hierarchy-title">Original hierarchy</h3><p class="tiny">Conditional on the observation variance \(\sigma^2\). The box shows unit [[k]] of the [[width]] units in layer [[l]].</p>
<svg id="hierarchy-graph" aria-label="Selected prior hierarchy"></svg>
<div id="vector-law">@selected-vector-laws@</div>
<section id="coordinate-graph" aria-labelledby="coordinate-title">
<h3 id="coordinate-title">The [[d]] coordinates of \(\mathbf w_{[[kl]]}\)</h3>
<p class="tiny">Given @selected-conditions@, the [[d]] coefficients below are independent. Each has the same conditional variance.</p>
<div class="coordinate-parent"><strong>Shared conditional variance</strong> @selected-variances@</div>
<ol class="coordinate-leaves">@selected-leaves@</ol></section>
@hierarchies@</div>
<p class="tiny">The unit-specific laws repeat over \(j=1,\ldots,[[width]]\). We use \(\operatorname{IG}(\alpha,\beta)\) with density proportional to \(t^{-\alpha-1}\exp(-\beta/t)\).</p></section>
@panel tail@
<span class="section-label">Prior on the unit scales · Equation (21)</span><header class="inspector-head"><h2>Tail probability for unit [[k]] in layer [[l]]</h2></header>
<p>Fix the layer parameters and take \(z\ge0\). The curves give the probability that \(\lambda_{[[kl]]}\) exceeds \(z\tau_{[[l]]}\). All [[width]] units in this layer have the same conditional tail probability under a given prior.</p>
\[S_{[[l]]}(z)=\Pr(\lambda_{[[kl]]}>z\tau_{[[l]]}\mid\tau_{[[l]]},\xi_{[[l]]}).\]
<p class="tiny">Condition on \(\tau_{[[l]]}\) alone for G and GL.</p>
<div class="law"><div>G \(\quad S_{[[l]]}(z)=\mathbf 1\{z<1\}\)</div><div>GL \(\quad S_{[[l]]}(z)=1-(2/\pi)\arctan(z)\)</div><div>GLT \(\quad S_{[[l]]}(z)=(1+\xi_{[[l]]}z)^{-1/\xi_{[[l]]}}\)</div></div>
<div class="range-row"><label for="tail-shape" id="tail-label">GLT tail shape \(\xi_{[[l]]}\)</label><output for="tail-shape" id="tail-value"></output></div>
<input type="range" id="tail-shape" min=".55" max="3" step=".05" value="1">
<svg id="tail-chart" class="chart" aria-label="Conditional tail probability by threshold"></svg>
<h3>At \(z=10\)</h3><div class="metric-list"><div class="metric"><span>G</span><strong id="tail-g"></strong></div><div class="metric"><span>GL</span><strong id="tail-gl"></strong></div><div class="metric"><span>GLT</span><strong id="tail-glt"></strong></div></div>
<p class="tiny">For G the probability drops to zero at \(z=1\), below the log axis. Under GLT, increasing \(\xi_{[[l]]}\) makes large unit scales more probable at fixed \(\tau_{[[l]]}\). In the joint prior, the distribution of \(\tau_{[[l]]}\) also depends on \(\xi_{[[l]]}\).</p></section>
@panel prune@
<span class="section-label">Fitted scales · Model comparison</span><header class="inspector-head"><h2>Pruning unit [[k]] in layer [[l]]</h2></header>
<p>Ghosh, Yao and Doshi-Velez group the same [[d]] incoming coefficients as our GL-BNN. In their notation \(\tau_{[[kl]]}\) is the unit’s local scale and \(\upsilon_{[[l]]}\) is the shared layer scale. Their post-fitting rule removes unit [[k]] if</p>
<div class="law">\[\Pr_q(\tau_{[[kl]]}\upsilon_{[[l]]}<\delta)>p_0.\]</div>
<p>Here \(q\) is their fitted variational distribution, \(\delta=10^{-3}\), and \(p_0=0.9\). In the grouped-horseshoe example, move the hypothetical fitted probability across the threshold.</p>
<div class="choice-group" role="group" aria-label="Pruning comparison"><button type="button" class="choice" data-method="ghosh" aria-pressed="true">Ghosh et al.</button><button type="button" class="choice" data-method="ours" aria-pressed="false">Fixed architecture</button></div>
<div class="range-row"><label for="prune-chance">Example fitted probability</label><output id="prune-value" for="prune-chance"></output></div><input type="range" id="prune-chance" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="95">
<p class="status" id="prune-result" aria-live="polite"></p>
<p>For our models, \(\Pr_q(\sigma\lambda_{[[kl]]}<\epsilon)\) summarizes mass near a small conditional weight SD. All [[width]] units remain in layer [[l]] of the fitted architecture. ReLU homogeneity allows an outgoing weight to compensate for a rescaling of the incoming weights and bias.</p></section>
</aside></div><section class="sources" id="sources"><h2>Paper &amp; references</h2>
<p>This illustration follows Figures 3–4 of <cite>Variational Learning of Bayesian Neural Networks with a family of global–local shrinkage priors</cite>. Its scale controls evaluate conditional prior laws. The <a href="../prediction/">fitted regression example</a> shows predictions from the R package.</p>
<ul><li><a href="https://jmlr.org/papers/v20/19-236.html">Ghosh, Yao &amp; Doshi-Velez (2019), §4.3</a> · Grouped horseshoe networks and posterior pruning.</li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1214/24-EJS2317">Lee, Zhao, Pati &amp; Mallick (2024)</a> · Tail-adaptive shrinkage for regression coefficients.</li></ul></section></main></div></body></html>
